#
# prima-materia — Graeco-Roman testimony
#
# Two claim types that the Indian and Buddhist files do not contain, and that
# the attestation modes exist to keep apart. The soul-journey reports are
# transmitted at second hand, by historians reporting what was said of men
# long dead. The healing report is transmitted by historians who appeal to
# living eyewitnesses. Neither is a practice manual, and neither is entered
# here as one.
#

pmt:GraecoRoman a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai ;
    rdfs:label "Graeco-Roman testimony"@en, "Griechisch-römisches Zeugnis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Greek and Latin historiographical and doxographical reports of extraordinary capacities, and of the argumentative literature that disputes them."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The book and chapter numbers are stable across the modern critical editions, which is why the loss is smaller here than elsewhere; it is still a loss, because it is unknown whether the passages were read in the original or in translation."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Herodotus, Histories; Tacitus, Historiae; Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus; Pliny, Naturalis Historia; Cicero, De divinatione; Artemidorus, Onirocriticon" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai, pmw:TacitiHistoriae, pmw:DivusVespasianus, pmw:NaturalisHistoria, pmw:DeDivinatione, pmw:ArtemidoriOnirocriticon ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#
# Added on 2026-09-21 with the first passage that argues instead of reporting.
# The file was not migrated before; AGENTS.md requires it of anyone who touches
# a tradition file.
#

pmw:HerodotiHistoriai a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Herodotus, Histories"@en, "Herodot, Historien"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at IV.13-16. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:TacitiHistoriae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tacitus, Historiae"@en, "Tacitus, Historien"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at IV.81. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:DivusVespasianus a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus"@en, "Sueton, Divus Vespasianus"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at chapter 7. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:NaturalisHistoria a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Pliny, Naturalis Historia"@en, "Plinius, Naturalis Historia"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at VII.174. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:DeDivinatione a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Cicero, De divinatione"@en, "Cicero, De divinatione"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at Book II, chapters XLII to XLVII, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, Bohn's Classical Library, London 1878. Read continuously; the remainder of both books was not read. Unlike the other four works of this corpus the edition is recoverable, because this passage was read in this run and not inherited."@en .

pmc:SoulJourney a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai, pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    rdfs:label "Journey of the soul apart from the body"@en, "Seelenreise ohne den Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the soul leaves the body and returns to it, reported of Aristeas of Proconnesus and of Hermotimus of Clazomenae."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16", "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" .

pmc:AristeasJourney a pm:Attributing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai, pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    rdfs:label "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"@en, "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16", "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" .

pmc:HealingByTouch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TacitiHistoriae, pmw:DivusVespasianus ;
    rdfs:label "Healing by touch"@en, "Heilung durch Berührung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The restoration of a withered hand and of sight, ascribed to the touch of a ruler in Alexandria."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81", "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7" .

pmc:VespasianHealing a pm:Attributing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TacitiHistoriae, pmw:DivusVespasianus ;
    rdfs:label "Healing by touch ascribed to Vespasian"@en, "Heilung durch Berührung, Vespasian zugeschrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a named ruler, in an account whose author remarks that those who were present still tell it although there is now no reward for the lie."@en ;
    skos:note "The remark about eyewitnesses is part of the ascription, not an endorsement of it. It records how the historian wanted the claim received."@en ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Vespasian ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:HealingByTouch ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81", "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7" .

pmc:Vespasian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TacitiHistoriae ;
    rdfs:label "Vespasian"@en, "Vespasian"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Roman emperor to whom the Alexandrian healings are ascribed by two independent historians."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" .

#
# Placement on the awareness axes.
#

pmc:SoulJourneyIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai, pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    rdfs:label "The journey of the soul is a displaced locus"@en, "Die Seelenreise ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situationGround "The reports have the soul leave the body and return to it. That is the criterion for this value, and it is what the accounts of Aristeas and Hermotimus turn on; whether anything of the sort occurred is a separate question and not this one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16", "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" .

#
# The argument against the calendar, and it argues from cases
#
# Two nodes from one passage, kept apart because they do different work. The
# first is the discriminating argument; the second is a claim about the
# evidence behind the practice, and it has a counterpart in a corpus that could
# not have known this one.
#

pmc:CiceroGeminiDiscrepantes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeDivinatione ;
    rdfs:label "One nativity and two fates, one fate and many nativities"@en, "Eine Geburtsstunde und zwei Geschicke, ein Geschick und viele Geburtsstunden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two cases the second book puts against the calculation of nativities, and they cut in opposite directions. First, two born at one moment whose lives diverge: Procles and Eurysthenes, twin kings of the Lacedaemonians, did not live the same number of years, for Procles died a year before his brother and much excelled him in the glory of his actions. Second, many born at many moments who meet one end: were all those slain in the battle of Cannae born under the same constellation, since they met with one and the same end? The passage adds a third of the same form, that a multitude is born at every moment and yet no one has ever been like Homer."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape is what this holding is short of. Neither case argues that the stars have no power; each takes the practice at its word, finds a pair the rule must classify alike, and points at the outcome. That is a criterion the practice itself supplies, applied to a case it cannot have chosen. The treatise also names the arguments as Panaetius's and not Carneades's, which places them inside the Stoa rather than outside it."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as what the transmission says and not as evidence. Nothing here is counted; two twins and one battle are instances adduced in an argument, and a protocol that wanted a rate would have to build it. SPEC §14 keeps such a work on the testimony side as long as it argues instead of counting, and this one argues."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Cicero, De divinatione II.43 and II.47, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, London: Bohn 1878, pp. 237-238 and 240" .

pmc:CiceroNullaTabulaObservationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeDivinatione ;
    rdfs:label "A claim of four hundred and seventy thousand years of records, and no record"@en, "Eine Behauptung von vierhundertsiebzigtausend Jahren Aufzeichnung, und keine Aufzeichnung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Some pretend, the passage says, that the Chaldean astrologers have verified the nativities of children by calculations and experiments in the cases of all the children born for four hundred and seventy thousand years; and this is a mistake, for had they been in the habit of doing so they would never have given up the practice, and as it is no author remains who knows of such a thing being done now or ever having been done. The chapter closes with the counterpart from the other side: how many Chaldean prophecies were repeated to Pompey, Crassus and Caesar, according to which not one of them was to die except in old age, in domestic felicity and perfect renown."@en ;
    skos:note "The argument is not that the record is bad but that it does not exist, and the ground given is behavioural: a practice that had such a record would still be keeping it. That is a test of a claim of verification by the presence of its own apparatus, and it is worth having as a form."@en ;
    skos:note "It has a counterpart in a corpus that could not have known it, and the pairing is the reason this node is separate. The rasāyana lesson of the Caraka states five times that its combinations are well tested by experience and reports no case; here a critic names the same gap in somebody else's practice and draws the consequence the Indian handbook does not draw about its own. Two corpora, one shape, and only one of them is looking at itself. See pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaPariksaSearch."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Cicero, De divinatione II.46-47, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, London: Bohn 1878, pp. 240-241" .

#
# The interpreter of dreams on whom to believe
#
# Opened on 2026-10-19 for the protocol at
# examinations/procedure-judged-by-its-own-working-tested.ttl, which looked
# outside the holding for a transmission comparing procedures of one kind for
# how well they work. The chapter compares the arts of divination and gives
# its ground in one clause that names both the arts and the men who work them.
# A practitioner's manual and not a historian's report, which is a third kind
# of witness in this file beside the reports and Cicero's argument.
#

pmw:ArtemidoriOnirocriticon a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Artemidorus of Daldis, Onirocriticon"@en, "Artemidor von Daldis, Onirokritikon"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at book II, chapter 69, in three versions. The Greek in the recension of Rudolf Hercher, Artemidori Daldiani Onirocriticon libri V ex recensione Rudolphi Hercheri, Lipsiae in aedibus B. G. Teubneri, as the title page in the full text gives it, read through a text layer that renders the Latin capitals of the title page in Greek letters; the year line is not legible there and the catalogue's 1864 is not used; identifier bub_gb_r8pJOYtS4W4C, pp. 161-162, where the Greek is uneven in the text layer and was read together with the apparatus beneath it. The German of Friedrich S. Krauss, Artemidoros aus Daldis, Symbolik der Träume (Wien, Pest, Leipzig: A. Hartleben, 1881), identifier BIUSante_55615, pp. 188-190. The Latin of Ianus Cornarius, De somniorum interpretatione libri quinque, printed at Basel by Hieronymus Froben and Nicolaus Episcopius in September 1539 as the colophon in the full text gives it, identifier desomnioruminter00arte, book II, chapter LXXIIII, which is Hercher's II.69. Two translators, Krauss and Cornarius, in two languages."@en ;
    skos:note "Not read: the rest of the five books. Seen and not entered: the end of the preceding chapter on dreams of flying, which gives correspondences between dream and outcome and none of the kinds the harvest owes. The Teubner edition of Roger Pack (1963) was located, identifier artemidori-daldiani-onirocriticon-1-5-1963-by-roger-pack, and its text layer carries no character of the Greek block among 549,139 letters, the Greek having been read into Latin letters, so it was not used."@en .

pmc:ArtemidorusAxiopistoi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArtemidoriOnirocriticon ;
    rdfs:label "Whose words in a dream are to be believed, and why"@en, "Wessen Worten im Traum zu glauben ist, und warum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The order of the trustworthy, whose words in a dream are to be believed and followed. First the gods, for lying is alien to gods; then priests, for they have the same honour among men as the gods; then kings and rulers, for what rules has the power of a god; then parents and teachers, for they are like gods, the one bringing us into life and the other teaching how to use it; then the diviners, but only those who are not cheats. Further the dead, for liars deceive from hope or from fear and the dead neither hope nor fear; children, for they do not yet know how to lie; the very old, for trustworthiness is shown by age; and the irrational animals. All other men lie, whatever they say, except those whose trustworthiness is evident and men of tried conduct; actors and chorus singers are unreliable to all by reason of their parts, and so are sophists, the poor, the galli and eunuchs, the last of whom signify false hopes even when they say nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule for reading dreams and not a procedure of its own, entered because it is the frame in which the comparison of the arts at pmc:ArtemidorusManteisMeApateones stands, and because its grounds are all properties of persons: their rank, their likeness to the gods, their want of a motive to lie, their age, their parts on a stage. Where the chapter ranks speakers it grounds the ranking in the speaker; where it ranks the arts the speakers practise it grounds the ranking in the art and in the speaker together."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Artemidorus, Onirocriticon II.69, in the Greek of R. Hercher (Leipzig: Teubner), pp. 161-162", "Artemidorus, Onirocriticon II.69, in the German of F. S. Krauss, Symbolik der Träume (Wien, Pest, Leipzig: A. Hartleben, 1881), pp. 188-190", "Artemidorus, De somniorum interpretatione II.74 (Hercher's II.69), in the Latin of Ianus Cornarius (Basel: Froben and Episcopius, 1539)" .

pmc:ArtemidorusManteisMeApateones a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArtemidoriOnirocriticon ;
    rdfs:label "Only the diviners who are not cheats: the true arts and the false"@en, "Nur die Seher, die keine Betrüger sind: die wahren Künste und die falschen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the trustworthy are the diviners, but of the diviners only those who are not cheats. Whatever Pythagoreans, physiognomists, dice-diviners, cheese-diviners, sieve-diviners, readers of the form, readers of the hand, basin-diviners and necromancers say is all to be held false and without foundation; for their arts are of that kind, and they themselves know not the least of divination itself, but by sorcery and deceit strip those who fall in with them. What remains as alone true is what is said by sacrificers, bird-diviners, star-gazers, observers of portents, dream-interpreters and liver-diviners; on the mathematicians who cast nativities the author defers judgement to a later place."@en ;
    skos:note "Two grounds in one clause, joined in the Greek by καὶ γὰρ ... καί: αἱ τέχναι αὐτῶν εἰσι τοιαῦται, their arts are of that kind, and αὐτῆς μὲν μαντικῆς οὐδὲ βραχὺ ἴσασι, γοητεύοντες δὲ καὶ ἀπατῶντες, they know nothing of divination itself and practise sorcery and deceit. Krauss keeps both (denn ihre Künste sind von der Art, und sie besitzen von der eigentlichen Mantik nicht die geringste Kenntniss, sondern ziehen durch Betrug und Schwindel ... aus), and so does Cornarius (nam ipsorum artes tales sunt, atque ipsi ne minimum quidem vaticinationis sciunt, verum praestigiis illudentes et defraudantes obvenientes exuunt). The first ground is not explained: nothing is said about what in the arts makes them false."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison is between procedures of one kind for whether they work, nine against six, and its ground is the procedure and the person together. The author counts his own art among the six. Cornarius's Latin has four of the six true arts, the bird-diviners, the star-gazers, the dream-interpreters and the liver-diviners, where Hercher's Greek and Krauss have six; the difference does not touch the ground."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    dcterms:source "Artemidorus, Onirocriticon II.69, in the Greek of R. Hercher (Leipzig: Teubner), p. 161 lines 20-27 and p. 162 lines 1-2", "Artemidorus, Onirocriticon II.69, in the German of F. S. Krauss, Symbolik der Träume (Wien, Pest, Leipzig: A. Hartleben, 1881), pp. 188-189", "Artemidorus, De somniorum interpretatione II.74 (Hercher's II.69), in the Latin of Ianus Cornarius (Basel: Froben and Episcopius, 1539)" .
